.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHDECK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshdeck
.Nd web configurator for nshd
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dv
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
answers the requests of a web page with what
.Xr nshd 8
answers.
.Xr httpd 8
passes it the requests over FastCGI.
A web user can do what that user's
.Xr nsh 8
can do, and no more:
.Nm nshd
checks the passwords and the permissions, and
.Nm
checks neither.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl s Ar socket
The socket, instead of
.Pa /var/www/run/nshdeck.sock .
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh SETUP
.Nm
needs the users _nshweb and _nshdeck, which may not be root or share a
uid, and the group www, which
.Xr httpd 8
runs as.
.Nm nshd
looks up _nshweb only when it starts, so restart
.Nm nshd
after creating _nshweb; until then it refuses every login.
.Pp
The socket is owned by root and the group www, with mode 0660.
In
.Xr httpd.conf 5 ,
with the page at
.Pa https://router/nshdeck/ :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
server "router" {
	listen on * tls port 443
	tcp nodelay
	connection max request body 3146752
	location "/nshdeck/api/*" {
		fastcgi socket "/run/nshdeck.sock"
	}
	location "/nshdeck/*" {
		root "/htdocs"
	}
}
.Ed
.Pp
Without
.Cm tcp nodelay
the terminal echoes what is typed in bursts, with pauses between them.
.Sh PROCESSES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  browser
     |  https
   httpd
     |  FastCGI, nshdeck.sock
   front ---- session ---- parent
                 |            |  connects
                 +--- nshd.sock, one connection for each user
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width "session"
.It parent
Starts as root, binds the socket, and starts the other two.
Then it runs as _nshweb, a member of the group nshadmin, and does one
thing: when session requests a connection, it connects to
.Nm nshd
and passes the connection on.
.Nm nshd
treats a client with _nshweb's uid as the web configurator, which has
no permissions until it logs in.
.It front
Runs as _nshdeck in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It reads the requests
.Xr httpd 8
sends, and checks each complete request before session sees it.
.It session
Runs as _nshweb in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It has a connection to
.Nm nshd
for each user logged in, and knows each user by the token in the user's
cookie.
It writes each answer as JSON.
It completes lines itself, with
.Xr nsh 8 Ns 's
code: it asks
.Nm nshd
for the command tables once, and for the names of interfaces, groups,
bridges and routing tables on each completion.
It asks
.Nm nshd
for each terminal a user opens.
.Nm nshd
starts
.Xr nsh 8
as the user and passes back its pseudo-terminal.
.El
.Pp
If one of them ends,
.Nm
ends.
.Sh LOGINS
Each user logs in with a name and password, which
.Nm nshd
checks, and has the permissions of the user's login class (see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) .
root may not log in.
.Pp
After 5 failed logins in a row for a name, or from an address, logins
for that name or from that address are refused for a second, without
checking the password.
Each further failure doubles the wait, up to 60 seconds.
While
.Nm nshd
checks a login, another login for the same name or from the same
address is refused, with a wait of 1 second, so each failure is counted
before the next login is checked.
The address is the client's address, as
.Xr httpd 8
gives it in REMOTE_ADDR.
An IPv6 address counts as its /64.
A login that succeeds resets the count for its name, and a count with
no failure for 15 minutes starts again from zero.
.Pp
Behind a proxy, every client has the proxy's address, so one user's
failures delay everyone.
An address can only delay a login, never allow one.
.Pp
A user with no event stream or terminal open and no request for 30
minutes is logged out.
When the user logs out, or
.Nm
reports that the login has ended, the page reloads, so that nothing the
last user saw remains.
.Sh THE PAGE
The page has seven workspaces, and a strip above them.
Each workspace but the terminal scrolls as a whole, and nothing in it
scrolls by itself.
A log that is followed, and the transcript of the lines typed, keep
their newest line in view as it comes, while it is in view.
.Ss Overview
Draws the device's ports as they appear on its front, each with a link
light and an activity light.
A device with more ports than fit in one row is drawn taller, its
ports in rows of equal length.
A readout shows how many ports are up, the numbers of interfaces and
addresses, and the last change.
The logical interfaces appear in groups by kind, each with what it is
on.
At its foot are the user's name and what each of the user's permissions
allows.
.Ss Network
Runs show commands and draws what they answer to be read: a time as
how long ago it was, a size in kilobytes to terabytes, a span in days
and hours, and each field's name in words.
The interfaces, addresses, routes, ARP and neighbor entries, VLANs,
bridges, DHCP leases, IPsec flows, logins and versions have views of
their own.
An interface's settings can be changed and staged into the candidate as
lines of configuration.
When the table of interfaces is too wide to fit beside the chosen
interface's settings, it leaves out each interface's MTU, routing domain
and type, which the settings' header shows; if it is still too wide,
the settings go under it.
.Ss Routing, security and services
Each lists its daemons, with a row for each routing table a daemon is
in.
A row shows whether the configuration enables the daemon and whether
it runs.
.Pp
For the chosen daemon, the workspace shows its rules under the list, as
text, which can be changed and staged into the candidate as the daemon's
edit command in
.Xr nsh 8
does.
Changes not yet staged remain until another daemon or routing table is
chosen.
For a daemon with no rules, it shows the command nsh starts the daemon
with.
The text is as tall as its lines, and the daemon's settings beside it
stay in view while they fit in the window.
.Pp
In configure, except configure terminal, it shows what the candidate
changes in the daemon.
It also lists each of the last ten saved configurations that changed
the daemon, with what each changed, and shows the daemon's live
operations.
.Pp
Some daemons have an editor, in tabs, which reads the rules' text and
writes changes back into it.
Its last tab shows the whole text: Rules for pf, Config for the
others.
A change rewrites only the words of the field it changes.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xr pf.conf 5
Its statements in order, with a form for each rule.
.It Xr bgpd.conf 5
Its peers, its networks and its filters.
For each peer: its state from
.Xr bgpctl 8 ,
the prefixes it accepts and advertises, its last errors, the filters
that apply to it, and its other settings in groups.
.It Xr ospfd.conf 5 , Xr ospf6d.conf 5 , Xr eigrpd.conf 5 , Xr ripd.conf 5 , \
Xr ldpd.conf 5 , Xr dvmrpd.conf 5
Their interfaces and global settings.
For each interface: the settings it sets and those it inherits from
the blocks around it.
Beside the editor are the neighbors and routes that their control
programs show in routing table 0.
.It Xr sshd_config 5 , Xr ntpd.conf 5 , Xr sasyncd.conf 5 , Xr rad.conf 5 , \
Xr relayd.conf 5 , Xr snmpd.conf 5 , Xr ldapd.conf 5 , Xr npppd.conf 5 , \
Xr smtpd.conf 5 , Xr ifstated.conf 5 , Xr iked.conf 5 , Xr dhcpd.conf 5 , \
Xr ipsec.conf 5
The statements of the rules, as fields.
.El
.Ss System and changes
Has six topics.
.Bl -tag -width "Live operations"
.It Changes
Where the mode of configure is chosen and lines of configuration are
typed, as
.Xr nsh 8
accepts them, beside a view of the changes, the candidate, the running
configuration or the saved configuration.
The page enters configure as the user logs in: the shared candidate for
a user with the admin permission, else a candidate of the user's own.
What the page changes anywhere goes into it.
Tab completes a line, and ? lists what may come next.
.It History
The saved configurations, any of which can be shown or loaded into the
candidate, and nshd's notices.
.It Logs
The logs checked, of daemon, messages and pf, as lines are added to
them, the newest at the foot.
Follow starts them again from their past.
.It Jobs and MOTD
Root's scheduled jobs and the message of the day, in the same way as
the daemons.
.It Live operations
Runs operations, each confirmed before it runs.
.It Errors
What nshd refused.
The strip shows how many.
.El
.Ss Terminal
Runs
.Xr nsh 8
as the user, and xterm.js draws it.
.Ss The strip
Has Compare, Validate and Commit, which show the changes, run
.Cm commit check ,
and run
.Cm commit .
It shows the warnings that
.Cm show rollback
prints, how many objects the candidate changes when it changes any, and
the time left to confirm an unconfirmed commit.
System's topics show the generation of the running configuration, and
when and by whom it was saved.
.Pp
A\(mi and A+, above Log out, make the page smaller or larger, and the
browser keeps the choice.
The page also grows with a window wider than 1440 pixels.
.Ss What the page keeps current
The page follows
.Nm nshd Ns 's
notices and logs as they arrive, and draws the interfaces again when
they change.
A log it follows begins with the lines in its last 64 kilobytes.
.Pp
While the overview or network is shown, the page requests the
interfaces every 5 seconds.
A port's activity light is on if the port counted packets since the
last request.
From the counters of two requests the page works out each interface's
rates, in and out, and keeps five minutes of them, which the interface
table draws as a small graph.
An interface's errors and drops are marked when they rose since the
last request, and the bar under a port is how full its line is.
While security is shown, a readout as the overview's shows
.Xr pf 4 Ns 's
states
and its rates, from
.Cm show statistics pf .
.Pp
The page alone keeps these rates: they begin when it is opened and are
gone when it is closed.
.Sh REQUESTS
The last part of a request's path names it.
A GET has its fields in its query, and a POST has them in a form body,
of type application/x-www-form-urlencoded.
Each field may be given only once.
.Pp
Every request except a stream,
.Cm events
or
.Cm terminal ,
must have the header
.Ql X-Nshdeck: 1 .
Only
.Nm Ns 's
own page can add it, because
.Nm
gives no other site permission to.
A stream cannot carry the header, as the browser asks for it, and
relies on the cookie alone.
.Pp
Every request except
.Cm login
needs the cookie that
.Cm login
sets.
.Bl -tag -width "configure"
.It Cm login
POST, with
.Ar user
and
.Ar password .
Logs the user in, and sets the cookie nshdeck, HttpOnly, Secure and
SameSite=Strict, to a token of 32 random bytes.
The answer has the user's name and permissions.
A login that must wait is refused, and the answer's
.Ar wait
is the number of seconds left (see
.Sx LOGINS ) .
.It Cm logout
POST.
Logs the user out.
.Nm nshd
forgets the login and the user's candidate of
.Cm configure private .
.It Cm whoami
GET.
Answers with the user's name and permissions.
.It Cm show
GET, with
.Ar words ,
the words of a show command in
.Xr nsh 8 ,
such as
.Ql interface
or
.Ql route inet 0 .
session sends the show to
.Nm nshd Ns 's
system process.
If the system process does not answer it, session sends it to
.Nm nshd Ns 's
show processes, as
.Xr nsh 8
does.
The answer has the records
.Nm nshd
sent, each an object with its kind and fields, and
.Ar fields :
for each kind, its fields in order, and what each holds: text, a
number, a name, names, an address, or a time, in seconds since 1970.
.It Cm config
GET, with
.Ar what :
running, startup, candidate, diff, or rollback, with or without
.Ar n ;
or changes, with
.Ar n .
The answer is the configuration, as text.
rollback without
.Ar n
lists the saved configurations.
changes is what saved configuration
.Ar n
changed from the one saved before it, as
.Cm show rollback Ar n Cm diff
in
.Xr nsh 8
shows it.
.It Cm configure
POST, with
.Ar mode :
private, shared, exclusive or terminal.
.It Cm line
POST, with
.Ar line :
a line of configuration, in configure.
.It Cm exit
POST.
Leaves configure, or the context a line entered.
.It Cm commit
POST, with
.Ar check
1 to check the candidate only,
.Ar minutes
for
.Cm commit confirmed ,
and a
.Ar comment .
.It Cm rollback
POST, with
.Ar n .
.It Cm op
POST, with
.Ar line ,
an operation such as
.Ql flush routes ,
and its
.Ar rtable .
.It Cm rules
GET, with
.Ar daemon ,
named as
.Xr nsh 8
names it, such as pf or bgp, and its
.Ar rtable .
The answer's
.Ar output
has the daemon's rules in the candidate.
The user must be in configure, but not in
.Cm configure terminal .
.It Cm setrules
POST, with
.Ar daemon ,
.Ar rtable
and
.Ar text ,
up to 1 MB.
Sets the daemon's rules in the candidate, as the daemon's
.Cm edit
in
.Xr nsh 8
does.
With no
.Ar text ,
the rules are removed.
Its body may be up to 3 MB.
At most 4 bodies of
.Cm setrules
and
.Cm stage
may arrive at once.
.It Cm stage
POST, with
.Ar interface ,
an interface's name;
.Ar bridge ,
1 if the interface is a bridge, else 0; and
.Ar text ,
up to 8 KB, the lines to run in the interface's mode, one to a line.
nshd enters the mode, runs each line until one fails, then leaves the
mode.
It runs no other request of the user's between them, from any page.
The answer's
.Ar results
says what nshd printed for each line that ran, entering and leaving
the mode included, and whether it succeeded.
Its body may be up to 25 KB.
.It Cm complete
GET, with
.Ar line ,
and
.Ar help
1 or 0.
Completes the line at its end, in the user's mode, as Tab does in
.Xr nsh 8 .
Each record is of kind insert, with the text to add to the line, or of
kind choice, with a word and its help, if it has one.
With
.Ar help
1, nothing is inserted and every choice is listed.
.It Cm events
GET, with
.Ar logs ,
names joined by commas: daemon, messages, authlog or pflog.
The answer is a stream of events, of type text/event-stream.
The events are
.Nm nshd Ns 's
notices and the lines of each log named, starting from its last lines.
The notices report commits, lines committed in configure terminal,
unconfirmed commits and their rollbacks,
.Cm configure exclusive
entered and left, and changes to interfaces and routes.
authlog needs the admin permission.
A comment is written to the stream every 30 seconds, so that
.Xr httpd 8
does not close it.
If the browser falls more than 1 MB behind, the oldest events not yet
sent to it are dropped, and a dropped event is sent in their place, so
that the page requests again what it shows.
.It Cm terminal
GET, with
.Ar rows
and
.Ar cols ,
each from 1 to 1000; they are 24 and 80 if not given.
Opens a terminal:
.Nm nshd
starts
.Xr nsh 8
on a pseudo-terminal, as the user.
The answer is a stream of events, like that of
.Cm events .
First comes a terminal event, with
.Ar n ,
the terminal's number, from 1 to 4.
Then out events carry what
.Xr nsh 8
writes, in base64, as it arrives.
At the end, an end event has
.Ar text ,
which says why the terminal ended.
The stream is sent only as fast as the browser reads it, and nothing
in it is dropped.
The terminal ends when its stream ends, when
.Xr nsh 8
ends, or when the user logs out.
A user may have at most 4 terminals.
.It Cm type
POST, with
.Ar terminal ,
its number,
.Ar keys ,
what was typed in it, up to 1023 bytes, and
.Ar rows
and
.Ar cols ,
for its new size.
What was typed is written to
.Xr nsh 8
as nsh reads it.
If more than 64 KB would then wait for nsh to read it, the request is
refused with 503.
.El
.Sh ANSWERS
Each answer except an event stream's is a JSON object.
An answer from
.Nm nshd
has
.Ar records ;
.Ar output ,
what
.Nm nshd
printed;
.Ar context ,
where the user is in configure;
.Ar status ;
and
.Ar ok .
It is sent with HTTP status 200, even when
.Nm nshd
refused the request.
.Pp
Other answers have an
.Ar error
and an HTTP status that says why:
.Bl -tag -width "XXX" -offset indent -compact
.It 400
a field that is not accepted, parameters that cannot be read, or a body
whose length is not its Content-Length
.It 401
the user is not logged in, or the login failed
.It 403
a request without the header
.It 404
an unknown request, or a
.Cm type
to a terminal that does not exist
.It 405
the wrong method
.It 413
a body over 8192 bytes, or over 3 MB for
.Cm setrules
.It 415
a POST that is not a form
.It 429
a login while its name or address must wait
.It 431
parameters over 16384 bytes
.It 503
.Nm nshd
does not answer, too many users are logged in, a user has too many
requests waiting or too many terminals, or too many rules are arriving
.El
.Sh LIMITS
A user's requests go to
.Nm nshd
one at a time, and at most 16 wait.
A long answer is read from
.Nm nshd
only as fast as the client reads it.
.Pp
At most 64 users may be logged in, and
.Nm
accepts at most 128 connections.
It counts the failed logins of at most 512 names and addresses.
It logs a login that fails or is refused at most once every 10 seconds
for its name and address, and each log line says how many logins it did
not log.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/www/run/nshdeck.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/nshdeck
the
.Xr rc.d 8
script, which starts
.Nm
.It Pa /var/www/run/nshdeck.sock
the socket
.Xr httpd 8
connects to
.It Pa /var/www/htdocs/nshdeck
the page
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr httpd.conf 5 ,
.Xr httpd 8 ,
.Xr nsh 8 ,
.Xr nshd 8 ,
.Xr rc.d 8
